{"authored" [{"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b175b0ab4ad058714b06a2abaf7cc561e52cfebf3ef81caada55893e769e68f2" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/state.json" "file_sha256" "316e47fbe9ef982da62b8c3e0919a1bca97ae1a218d29b4c152e2986ca3f89ef" "basis" 1464 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040648/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-71270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $y=-2$ and $x=z-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $y=-2$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x=z-1$ (or $x-z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3"} {"question" "q-71270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,1,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,0,-1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,0,-1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $y-z=1$ and $2x+z=3$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,-2,-2⟩.$\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y-z=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2x+z=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and  III are true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-71464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $3x-2y-18=0$ and $2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},0,{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives \n\n$$\n3x-0-18=0\\,⟹\\,x=6,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0-3z+18=0\\,⟹\\,z=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨6,0,6⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $3x-2y-18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,-2,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+9j+6k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨6,0,6⟩+t⟨6,9,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6-6=0,$ and $b=⟨6,9,6⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=-3,\\,b=⟨9,6,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨3,9,3⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=3,\\,b=⟨6,3,6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=6,\\,b=⟨6,6,3⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129202" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}-{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨6,9,6⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The intersection of the planes $2x-3z=0$ and $2y-3z-6=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0=0\\,⟹\\,x=0,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n2y-0-6=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,3,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-3z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $2y-3z-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =6i+6j+4k \\\\  & =⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,3,0⟩+t⟨6,6,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0+3=3,$ and $b=⟨6,6,4⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨6,6,-4⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=12,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,4,-6⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩"] ["math" "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨4,6,6⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129215" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨6,6,4⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the two planes $x-3y+2z=0$ and $x-2y+5=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The normal vectors of the planes are parallel.\n- The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$\n- The planes intersect on a line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+5=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-2,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =4i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d2853dc55564501041711ebd56085a7140d70b3a8ba74621028d2f1b5cd1cb3"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"} {"question" "q-129241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14054673/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d8603d099470df6c04b4648a71c73b6f0820183612efd9da70f351ec43a7729"}] "usage" {"q-129202" []} "basis" 1799 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186309479 17592186309480 17592186309481 17592186309482 17592186309483] "immutable_fields" [[17592186309478 :answer-field/correct] [17592186309478 :answer-field/id] [17592186309478 :answer-field/key] [17592186309478 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
